    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Changes in intracellular calcium are a hallmark of many cellular processes, including spiking activity in neurons, activation of G-protein coupled receptor cascades, or metabolic regulation in the mitochondria. It would be beneficial to have a technology that can rapidly and stably label cells exhibiting an increase in intracellular calcium following some stimulus. These cells could then be processed and analyzed to reveal calcium-dependent changes in gene expression or protein localization. There are various biotechnological tools that label activated cells after elevated intracellular calcium with a stable read-out, such as photo- conversion of a calcium-sensitive fluorophore (CaMPARI) or a calcium-dependent transcriptional reporter (FLARE); however, these tools either have limited sensitivity or require approximately 9 hours to provide the transcriptional read-out of activity. An efficient cell labeling tool that generates a stable signal within minutes would benefit researchers interested in investigating dynamic processes in cells that take place quickly after periods of high intracellular calcium. Here, we present instaFLARE, a novel technology that addresses the need for rapid and stable reporter expression; by using a reconstitutable, split infrared fluorescent protein (Split-IFP) instead of a transcription-initiated reporter gene, it is possible to engineer a tool that has much faster kinetics of reading out cells undergoing high intracellular calcium. instaFLARE’s faster time scales of activation and cell labeling has been tested by introducing the engineered proteins into HEK293 cells, exposing the cells to blue light and calcium at the same time, followed by subsequent imaging of the reconstituted IFP reporter at different time stamps after light and calcium introduction. In the future with further engineering, instaFLARE will allow for quick labeling of activated cells, which will enable researchers to investigate dynamic changes in the cell after rises in calcium that would otherwise be undetectable.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
